## Task: Implement static-retrieval-mrl-en-v1 ONNX backend (baseline-only unless eligible)

### IMPORTANT
Only implement if model (or post-2025-10 variant) appears in embedding_candidates_2025_11+.json as eligible. Otherwise baseline-only.

## Purpose
Provide a static-embedding baseline and validate Matryoshka (MRL) truncation pipeline.
static-retrieval-mrl-en-v1 was reported as 397x faster than mpnet while supporting
MRL dimension truncation (768 → 256 → 128 → 64 with graceful quality degradation).

## Architecture
```rust
pub struct StaticMrlEmbedder {
    session: ort::Session,       // ONNX runtime session
    tokenizer: tokenizers::Tokenizer,
    native_dims: usize,          // 768 (full dimension)
    target_dims: Option<usize>,  // MRL truncation target (e.g., 256)
    name: String,
}

impl Embedder for StaticMrlEmbedder {
    fn embed_batch(&self, texts: &[&str]) -> Result<Vec<Vec<f32>>> {
        // 1. Tokenize all texts
        let encodings: Vec<_> = texts.iter()
            .map(|t| self.tokenizer.encode(*t, true))
            .collect::<Result<Vec<_>>>()?;

        // 2. Pad to max length in batch
        let max_len = encodings.iter().map(|e| e.len()).max().unwrap_or(0);
        let batch_size = encodings.len();

        // 3. Build input tensors
        let input_ids = build_padded_tensor(&encodings, max_len, 0);
        let attention_mask = build_attention_mask(&encodings, max_len);

        // 4. Run ONNX inference
        let outputs = self.session.run(ort::inputs![
            "input_ids" => input_ids,
            "attention_mask" => attention_mask,
        ]?)?;

        // 5. Extract embeddings (static model: single forward pass, fast)
        let embeddings_raw = outputs[0].extract_tensor::<f32>()?;

        // 6. Mean pool over token positions (respecting attention mask)
        let mut results = Vec::with_capacity(batch_size);
        for i in 0..batch_size {
            let mut pooled = mean_pool_with_mask(
                &embeddings_raw, i, max_len, self.native_dims, &attention_mask
            );

            // 7. MRL truncation if target_dims specified
            if let Some(target) = self.target_dims {
                pooled.truncate(target);
            }

            // 8. L2 normalize (must re-normalize after truncation!)
            l2_normalize(&mut pooled);
            results.push(pooled);
        }

        Ok(results)
    }

    fn dimensions(&self) -> usize {
        self.target_dims.unwrap_or(self.native_dims)
    }

    fn model_name(&self) -> &str { &self.name }

    fn supports_mrl(&self) -> bool { true }

    fn truncate(&self, embeddings: &[Vec<f32>], target_dim: usize) -> Result<Vec<Vec<f32>>> {
        assert!(target_dim <= self.native_dims, "Cannot expand beyond native dims");
        Ok(embeddings.iter().map(|emb| {
            let mut truncated: Vec<f32> = emb[..target_dim].to_vec();
            l2_normalize(&mut truncated);
            truncated
        }).collect())
    }
}
```

## Model Files
- Model: model.onnx (static model, relatively small)
- Tokenizer: tokenizer.json
- Config: config.json (native dimensions, supported MRL dims)
- Cache path: ~/.cache/xf/models/static-retrieval-mrl-en-v1/

## Key Considerations
- "Static" means the transformer weights are frozen/distilled into a simpler architecture
- Much faster than full transformer inference but quality trades off
- MRL support: embeddings can be truncated to lower dims with controlled quality loss
- Re-normalization after truncation is CRITICAL (truncated vectors are not unit length)
- The 397x speed claim needs independent verification in our benchmark

## MRL Dimension Analysis
The benchmark should test at multiple truncation levels:
- 768 dims (full, best quality)
- 512 dims (moderate truncation)
- 256 dims (aggressive, good speed/quality tradeoff)
- 128 dims (very aggressive, significant quality loss expected)
- 64 dims (extreme, mostly for testing graceful degradation)

## Location
src/models/static_mrl.rs

## Validation and Tests
### Unit tests
- Tokenization produces expected token sequence
- Mean pooling respects attention mask (padding ignored)
- MRL truncation produces vectors of correct target dimension
- Re-normalization after truncation produces unit vectors
- truncate() with target > native_dims returns error
- Empty text input handled gracefully
- Batch of 1 matches individual embedding
- supports_mrl() returns true

### Integration tests
- Embed tiny corpus at full dimensions, verify:
  - Vectors normalized (L2 norm within 1e-6 of 1.0)
  - Dimensions match native_dims
  - Deterministic (same input → same output)
- Embed same corpus at 256 dims via MRL, verify:
  - Vectors still normalized after truncation
  - Cosine similarity ordering preserved (MRL guarantee)
  - Quality degradation is bounded (not catastrophic)
- Speed comparison: static vs transformer on same corpus

### E2E script (detailed logging)
- scripts/embedders/static_mrl_e2e.sh
  - Logs: model size, ONNX session creation, first-embed cold start
  - Logs: RSS before/after model load
  - Logs: throughput at batch sizes 1, 10, 100, 1000
  - Logs: MRL quality at each dim level (cosine sim with full-dim reference)
  - Tests: verify 397x speed claim vs mpnet baseline
  - Tests: MRL truncation at 768, 512, 256, 128, 64 dims
  - Verify: re-normalization produces unit vectors at all dim levels

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Eligibility confirmed from bd-e9kz
